WEBVTT

00:00:15.475 --> 00:00:35.883
<v Casey>Welcome to Cariboo Signals, an AI-generated review of the latest tech news and ideas that impact our rural communities — I'm Casey. It's Friday, September 11th, 2026. Coming to you from the Cariboo, on lands that have been cared for by the Secwépemc, Tŝilhqot'in, and Dakelh peoples since time immemorial. Today's theme is Wild Spaces &amp; Outdoor Life.

00:00:36.883 --> 00:01:12.091
<v Riley>And I'm Riley. We're talking backcountry today — the trails, the tools, and the wildfire risk that come bundled together when you actually live out here instead of just visiting it. Quick weather sweep first: Horsefly Lake is mainly clear this morning, around 7 degrees, high of 18, low of 5, with a light drizzle expected through the day and roughly a 32 percent chance of precipitation. 100 Mile House is sitting around 6 with clear skies, Williams Lake around 8 and clear, Quesnel around 7 and partly cloudy, and Redstone around 6, also partly cloudy.

00:01:12.509 --> 00:01:14.637
<v Casey>Plenty on the list today. Let's get into it.

00:01:16.037 --> 00:01:22.365
<v Casey>Four stories close to home this morning, and a couple of them have real teeth. We'll start in 100 Mile House.

00:01:23.484 --> 00:01:48.532
<v Riley>A black bear that had been circling residential streets in 100 Mile House for weeks was euthanized Thursday after conservation officers decided it had become too comfortable around people. It had been getting into garbage bins repeatedly and wasn't moving off when residents were nearby, which is the threshold conservation officers use to call a bear food-conditioned rather than just passing through.

00:01:49.032 --> 00:01:52.336
<v Casey>Once a bear loses its fear of people, that's usually a one-way door.

00:01:53.564 --> 00:02:22.924
<v Riley>It is. Relocation success rates for food-conditioned bears are low — they either find their way back to the same neighborhood or repeat the same behavior somewhere new, and conservation officers know that going in. The rural angle here is unglamorous but real: garbage management is wildlife management. Every unsecured bin on a 100 Mile House street is effectively a vote for this outcome, and it's the same story every fall in towns this size across the Cariboo.

00:02:24.124 --> 00:02:39.500
<v Casey>Over in Quesnel, RCMP arrested four people Thursday morning after a stolen white Ford F350 rammed police cruisers during an attempted traffic stop. The driver was a 31-year-old man, and the passengers were a 29-year-old woman and two girls aged fifteen.

00:02:40.143 --> 00:02:42.807
<v Riley>Any word yet on what charges are actually being laid?

00:02:44.131 --> 00:03:04.099
<v Casey>Not released as of Thursday afternoon — Quesnel RCMP say the investigation is still active. It's a straightforward property-crime story on its face, but ramming a cruiser during a stop pushes it into something more serious than a stolen-vehicle report, and it's the kind of incident that ties up patrol resources for a full shift in a detachment that size.

00:03:05.299 --> 00:03:32.059
<v Riley>The Union of B.C. Municipalities holds its annual convention this month, and Williams Lake's delegation is heading in with a specific list of grievances. The Tribune reports UBCM plans to press the province directly on funding cuts and on what local governments are calling a pattern of secrecy — major decisions made in Victoria without local consultation, then handed down to municipalities as done deals with no room to negotiate terms.

00:03:33.336 --> 00:03:42.024
<v Casey>Which is the same complaint we heard about wildfire recovery dollars a few weeks back — money gets announced, but it's hard to trace once it actually lands in a community.

00:03:43.339 --> 00:04:07.419
<v Riley>Right, and UBCM's specific ask this year is for clearer reporting requirements built into provincial funding agreements themselves, not just larger cheques with no accountability attached. Rural and northern delegates argue their communities are hit harder by this because they don't have the staff capacity that a city like Vancouver has to chase down where money actually went after the announcement.

00:04:08.619 --> 00:04:21.019
<v Casey>And in Williams Lake, ICBC announced it's replacing all 140 intersection cameras across the province with updated hardware — the speed and red-light camera systems installed at the highest-collision intersections in B.C.

00:04:21.552 --> 00:04:24.560
<v Riley>Do we know if any Williams Lake intersections made that list?

00:04:25.920 --> 00:04:50.928
<v Casey>The Tribune's reporting doesn't name specific locations for this rollout, but ICBC says the new cameras are meant to catch a broader range of violation types than the current generation, which apparently has known blind spots around certain turning movements. For a city the size of Williams Lake, even a single upgraded intersection is a meaningful chunk of the local traffic-safety picture, given how few high-volume intersections there actually are.

00:04:52.127 --> 00:05:21.255
<v Riley>From cameras watching intersections to the air itself getting harder to breathe. Scientific American reported this week that El Niño-driven wildfires in Indonesia have sickened an estimated 100,000 people so far this year. The fires are burning through peatlands specifically, and peatland fires release far more particulate matter per hectare than a typical forest fire because the fuel is essentially compressed organic soil, not standing timber.

00:05:22.635 --> 00:05:30.459
<v Casey>Peat fires smoulder underground for weeks at a time. You don't really put them out. You wait them out, sometimes through an entire dry season.

00:05:31.564 --> 00:05:51.508
<v Riley>That's the ugly mechanic of it. And Europe's fire season hasn't wrapped up either — Wildfire Today reports extreme and very extreme fire-danger conditions are still forecast across southern, central, and eastern Europe, with Spain flagged as one of the highest-risk zones, even after a brief cooling spell earlier in the month.

00:05:52.674 --> 00:06:00.698
<v Casey>Two continents, and it's the same underlying story: a warming baseline is turning what used to be an unusually bad fire season into an ordinary one.

00:06:01.598 --> 00:06:20.270
<v Riley>A bit farther afield — the Committee to Protect Journalists, together with eight partner organizations, sent an open letter this week to Pakistan's prime minister over what they're calling an alarming deterioration in press freedom there, citing detentions and restrictions on reporters covering the government directly.

00:06:21.694 --> 00:06:36.726
<v Casey>Worth flagging here because a functioning press is part of how a community finds out a wildfire's coming, or a river's rising, before it shows up at the front door. When that channel gets squeezed by a government, people lose the lead time that actually saves lives.

00:06:37.926 --> 00:07:03.166
<v Riley>Back to backcountry terrain specifically — ScienceDaily published research this week out of a University of California team that tracked 4,500 animals across dozens of species using GPS collars, and found something that apparently surprised the researchers themselves: our physical presence alone reshapes where wildlife moves and lives, sometimes as strongly as roads, cities, or other permanent development.

00:07:04.616 --> 00:07:09.928
<v Casey>So it's not just the highway changing where the animals go. It's us, walking the trail next to it.

00:07:11.343 --> 00:07:34.399
<v Riley>That's the finding — animals in the study reacted to hikers and campers as a distinct pressure, separate from and sometimes larger than their reaction to the built infrastructure those people arrived on. For a region actively debating how hard to promote its own backcountry to outside visitors, that's a data point worth sitting with before the next tourism campaign gets drafted.

00:07:35.299 --> 00:07:49.155
<v Casey>A run of quick ones to close out. ScienceAlert reports a new study arguing Alzheimer's risk may originate outside the brain entirely, pointing to broader immune and vascular factors that researchers say have been underweighted in past research.

00:07:50.565 --> 00:08:05.357
<v Riley>And a separate ScienceAlert piece — an experimental one-dose treatment cut so-called bad cholesterol by 52 percent in its first human trial, published in the New England Journal of Medicine. Early stage, but a striking number for a single dose.

00:08:06.536 --> 00:08:17.440
<v Casey>Lenovo showed off a rollable laptop concept alongside some colorful new IdeaPads this week — part of a broader industry push, apparently, to make laptops look like something other than beige office furniture.

00:08:18.706 --> 00:08:35.858
<v Riley>In Texas and Florida, Republican governors are pulling back on Flock Safety's license-plate-reading cameras. Governor Abbott directed police departments to stop spending state money on them, even as police departments argue the cameras help solve property crime and locate stolen vehicles.

00:08:35.758 --> 00:08:39.462
<v Casey>Bipartisan backlash on surveillance tech. That doesn't happen often.

00:08:40.945 --> 00:08:50.921
<v Riley>Four teens in Lethbridge, ages 13 to 15, were charged earlier this month after one allegedly brandished an airsoft pistol during a confrontation at a shopping mall.

00:08:52.045 --> 00:09:02.981
<v Casey>And in Texas, electric air taxis got the green light this week for test flights under a new federal program, kicking off before the rules that are actually supposed to govern them have been finalized.

00:09:03.881 --> 00:09:27.881
<v Riley>One more before the spotlight, and it's a heavier one. The Atlantic published a long piece this week examining why the man accused of masterminding the September 11th attacks was never convicted, arguing the George W. Bush administration's decision to circumvent established legal process in pursuing him ultimately backfired in the courts and delayed any verdict for a quarter century.

00:09:29.223 --> 00:09:42.679
<v Casey>Hard subject on a day like today, but the throughline is procedural, not emotional — bypassing due process didn't just raise moral objections at the time, it failed on its own practical terms. Twenty-five years later, there still isn't a conviction.

00:09:43.195 --> 00:09:45.443
<v Riley>Worth sitting with, on the anniversary itself.

00:09:45.943 --> 00:09:51.607
<v Casey>That's the roundup. Before the Deep Dive, a word about an organization doing real work in our own backyard.

00:09:53.007 --> 00:10:21.727
<v Riley>Scout Island Nature Centre in Williams Lake is volunteer-run through the Williams Lake Field Naturalists, and if you've got a kid who likes birds, ponds, or getting muddy for a good reason, it's worth knowing about. Their Salmon Trip sends students out to Gavin Lake for three days of actual fieldwork — brood stock collection, stream surveys, hands-on salmon habitat research, not a worksheet version of any of it. Applications are open now at scoutisland-dot-C-A.

00:10:22.337 --> 00:10:29.937
<v Casey>That's the kind of program that teaches a skill by putting a kid's hands in a stream, rather than talking about the stream from a classroom.

00:10:30.299 --> 00:10:33.259
<v Riley>Which is a decent bridge into what we're covering next.

00:10:34.659 --> 00:10:56.603
<v Riley>Ben Dhiman won the UTMB last week in eighteen hours, sixteen minutes, and twenty-nine seconds — the fastest anyone has ever completed the Mont-Blanc course. He beat the previous course record, set by Britain's Tom Evans just last year, by over an hour. In elite ultrarunning, records typically fall by a couple of minutes at a time. An hour is not a normal jump.

00:10:57.103 --> 00:11:09.191
<v Casey>A hundred and six miles, through the night, over multiple mountain passes, in under nineteen hours total. That's the kind of number that should make you ask what actually changed between last year's field and this one.

00:11:09.813 --> 00:11:41.381
<v Riley>Training science changed, and it changed fast. GPS-guided pacing plans that tell a runner exactly when to hold back on a climb, real-time fueling protocols dialed in from sports-science labs, shoe technology that's essentially a carbon-fiber spring built into the sole — all of it lets someone fully exploit terrain that used to just break athletes by mile seventy. Dhiman had run UTMB three times before this without a win. He was runner-up last year. This year the training and the tools finally lined up at once, in the same race.

00:11:42.054 --> 00:11:55.414
<v Casey>Or the course changed. The article itself notes his time isn't officially recognized as a course record, because organizers altered the route this year. So we're comparing a fast run to a slightly different mountain than the one Tom Evans ran.

00:11:56.091 --> 00:12:08.467
<v Riley>The route did shift. But even accounting for that, a jump this size in a field this deep points to something structural in how the top of the sport trains now — not one gifted athlete having a single good night.

00:12:08.966 --> 00:12:35.870
<v Casey>Which raises the real question here. What happens to the word "wild" in wild-space racing when the whole support apparatus is built specifically to remove every variable that made the terrain wild in the first place? Weather modeling. Satellite pacing feedback. Sponsor-engineered footwear designed in a lab. At some point you're not testing a person against a mountain anymore. You're testing one support system against another support system, and the mountain is just the venue.

00:12:36.381 --> 00:12:47.085
<v Riley>That's the tension every one of these tools runs into eventually. A satellite communicator doesn't replace a runner's judgment out on the course. It changes what that judgment gets applied to.

00:12:47.767 --> 00:13:06.175
<v Casey>Except UTMB is sponsored by a major shoe and apparel company, and Dhiman races for them. The gear arms race here isn't a hypothetical side effect. It's the actual business model funding the sport. The question isn't whether the tech helped him win. It's whether what people are watching is still the same event it was twenty years ago.

00:13:07.075 --> 00:13:42.011
<v Riley>Here's where the optimist case gets harder to hold, though. Go about three hundred kilometers west of Mont-Blanc's tidy aid stations and you land on the Wait Creek wildfire, near Ta Ta Creek and Kimberley in the East Kootenay. That fire grew past 1,300 hectares and triggered an evacuation order covering close to a thousand properties — 972 dwellings, by the Regional District's count, including a number of campsites. No amount of GPS pacing or carbon-fiber shoe helps anyone there. The wild space just becomes dangerous, full stop, on a timeline nobody can train around.

00:13:42.689 --> 00:14:03.633
<v Casey>Which is the counterweight to every "technology is making wild spaces safer" argument this show tends to reach for. The people affected weren't out there recreating extra carefully — some were just living there, or camping for the weekend, and the ground under them turned dangerous with almost no lead time they could have prepared for no matter how good their gear was.

00:14:04.176 --> 00:14:33.216
<v Riley>Except the emergency response itself is the part worth examining closely. The Regional District of East Kootenay didn't issue one static order and walk away — they expanded the evacuation area tactically as the fire grew, and then shrank the affected zone again as crews made progress on containment. That's real-time fire monitoring translating directly into a boundary line that actually tracks the fire's behavior, redrawn twice in about two weeks rather than left as one fixed line drawn on day one.

00:14:33.883 --> 00:14:46.803
<v Casey>Shrinking an evacuation zone after nearly a thousand households have already had their week upended isn't really a technology win, though. It's damage control functioning as intended, eventually, after the disruption's already happened.

00:14:47.302 --> 00:15:06.918
<v Riley>Both things are true at once. The monitoring tech didn't stop the fire from starting or spreading. But it kept people from being displaced longer than necessary, and it gave emergency managers a fire boundary they trusted enough to move twice within two weeks instead of leaving families in limbo on outdated information.

00:15:07.018 --> 00:15:23.882
<v Riley>The Okanagan Forest Task Force raised almost this exact tension a few weeks back, asking the province for help keeping backcountry safe — more patrol capacity, better trail signage, faster emergency response for the stretches of terrain that sit past cell coverage entirely.

00:15:24.442 --> 00:15:43.098
<v Casey>And what they're describing, if you read it closely, is a capacity gap, not a technology gap. The tools to monitor a fire's edge or track a lost hiker's beacon signal already exist and mostly work. What's missing across most of the Cariboo is enough trained people on the ground to act on what those tools are telling them, fast enough to matter.

00:15:43.998 --> 00:16:19.974
<v Riley>Which brings it back to the question underneath both of these stories. What happens to the actual skill of reading a landscape once the tools get good enough that a person doesn't strictly need that skill anymore? A satellite communicator means you don't have to read the sky before a storm the way someone did forty years ago, watching cloud behavior and wind shift for hours before committing to a route. GPS pacing means an elite ultrarunner doesn't have to build the felt sense of their own physical limits the hard way, over years of blowing up on climbs and learning exactly where that line sits.

00:16:20.545 --> 00:16:38.001
<v Casey>Some of that lost friction is fine to lose. Nobody's mourning the era when getting lost in the bush without a map was a routine way to die out here. But there's a real difference between friction that was just drudgery worth shedding, and friction that was the only mechanism anyone ever actually learned the terrain through.

00:16:38.682 --> 00:17:08.442
<v Riley>That's the distinction that matters most here. Reading a river before you cross it. Knowing which slope still holds snow past what month, based on years of watching it. Knowing a particular stand of timber because you've walked it a hundred times in every season. None of that transfers through an app, no matter how good the app's mapping layer is. A GPS track shows a hiker exactly where the trail goes. It doesn't teach anyone why the trail goes there, or what happens fifty meters off it.

00:17:08.942 --> 00:17:37.950
<v Casey>The Tŝilhqot'in National Government put out an advisory this week asking hunters not to shoot at anything they haven't positively identified — a direct response to collared wildlife and to increased traffic through the backcountry during hunting season. That's the embodied-knowledge gap showing up in real time, not as an abstraction. More people are out on that land with less accumulated read on it than the hunters who came before them, and a Nation is having to issue a public safety notice specifically because of that gap.

00:17:38.519 --> 00:18:12.439
<v Riley>Though that cuts both ways on the tech question. Those collars exist so TNG can track and manage wildlife populations with a precision that wasn't possible even a decade ago. The same technology sitting at the center of the "easier access" problem is also the tool letting them respond to it in real time. We got into this a couple weeks back with the guardian programs — the collars extend what a guardian can watch across a huge territory. They don't replace someone who's walked that land long enough to read a track in the mud without needing a receiver.

00:18:12.951 --> 00:18:31.759
<v Casey>Extending capacity and replacing a skill are not the same outcome, and that's the exact gap nobody seems to be measuring. Nobody's tracking whether the person wearing the guardian badge in fifteen years still has the embodied read on the terrain their predecessors had, or whether they just have a very good piece of tracking software and a truck.

00:18:32.259 --> 00:18:41.867
<v Riley>So what would you actually want measured, then? Because "somebody should be tracking this" is easy to say on a podcast and hard to build into an actual program.

00:18:42.475 --> 00:19:01.707
<v Casey>Something as concrete as an apprenticeship requirement attached to any program that hands out the technology itself. A season spent walking the ground with someone who already knows it, before the collar data or the satellite beacon becomes the primary interface a person relies on. Not a grant line item. A condition of the tech being issued at all.

00:19:02.341 --> 00:19:25.709
<v Riley>That's workable, and it's not hypothetical — Scout Island's Salmon Trip is basically that model already, at a much smaller scale. Three days at Gavin Lake doing brood stock collection and stream surveys by hand, not off a dashboard reading. Kids come out of that program actually knowing what a healthy stream bed looks like, because they stood in one and did the count themselves.

00:19:26.209 --> 00:19:38.097
<v Casey>Which is small, and it doesn't scale up to backcountry search-and-rescue or wildfire response on its own. But it's evidence the apprenticeship model works at whatever size a community program can actually run it.

00:19:38.638 --> 00:20:03.470
<v Riley>The honest read on the UTMB story and the Wait Creek story side by side is that technology is making the wild both safer to access and faster to respond to in a genuine emergency, and it's letting the underlying skill of reading the land atrophy in the people using it, at the same time. Both of those are true simultaneously. The disagreement between us isn't really about the facts. It's about which one worries you more.

00:20:03.999 --> 00:20:17.471
<v Casey>The second one worries me more, because nobody's built a mechanism to notice it's happening until the last person who could read a particular stretch of river without checking a device retires, and nobody trained under them before that happened.

00:20:18.371 --> 00:20:30.715
<v Riley>If you've got a story about a program teaching backcountry skills the old way — hands in the water, boots on the ground — or a correction on anything we got wrong today, the email is feedback at cariboo signals dot C-A.

00:20:31.329 --> 00:20:39.921
<v Casey>And if you're one of the people who still reads a river without checking a phone first, we'd like to hear from you too. That's a rarer skill than most people think.

00:20:40.207 --> 00:20:41.495
<v Riley>Enjoy your weekend, Cariboo.

00:20:41.675 --> 00:20:42.379
<v Casey>See you Monday.